THE COLLISIONS ARE LIVE AND CAUSING DUPLICATE SCHEMA 2026-08-22. AC7's audit predicted ten collisions with real prerendered routes; four of the sampled ones now serve FAQPage TWICE in production - /events/today, /events/this-weekend, /events/date-night and /events/altoona - because the React route and the published pSEO page for the same slug each emit one. public.pseo_pages has 25 published rows under /events/ alone. See WEB-SEO-008, which had concluded the duplication was a stale GSC report.
  This raises the cost of leaving the collision unresolved: it is no longer a hypothetical ranking risk, it is invalid structured data on live URLs that GSC is already flagging.

RE-MEASURED 2026-08-22 (claude/prd-p1-loop). THE TITLE IS NO LONGER TRUE, and what replaced it is worse. ACs 1, 2, 4 and 7 are done; 5 and 6 are not; and the 244 shipped pages are live in a state that actively hurts.

AC1/AC2 DONE, contrary to the AC text 'It never queries pseo_pages'. scripts/generate-dynamic-sitemaps.ts:356 queries pseo_pages, :401 writes public/sitemap-pseo.xml and :513 registers it in the sitemap index. Verified against PRODUCTION, not the repo: https://desmoinesinsider.com/sitemap-pseo.xml is HTTP 200, 44,215 bytes, 244 <loc> entries, and the live sitemap.xml index lists it alongside the other seven. robots.txt lists it too. The committed public/sitemap-*.xml files are stale by design - they are regenerated inside npm run build - so reading the repo copy (1 URL, the error fallback) understates it badly.
AC4 DONE: PSEO_URL_CAP = 300 with a MIN_QUALITY_SCORE floor of 0.6. 244 published pages sit under the cap.

INVENTORY: public.pseo_pages holds 244 rows, ALL is_published = true, average quality_score 0.8. published_at is NULL on every row, so any future logic keying on published_at will see nothing.

THE SERIOUS FINDING - ALL 244 SERVE THE HOMEPAGE. Fetched with a GPTBot UA and no JS on 2026-08-22, /things-to-do/downtown, /guide/downtown and /guide/east-village each return HTTP 200 with 170,934 bytes, title 'Things to Do in Des Moines This Weekend', and
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://desmoinesinsider.com/">
So the site is currently SUBMITTING 244 URLs to search engines whose non-JS response instructs crawlers to treat every one of them as the homepage. This story worried that a page-count spike would 'read as suspicious'; the live situation is worse than a spike - it is 244 sitemap-submitted duplicates of /. Identical to the WEB-SEO-006 entity finding, already shipped at scale here.

THE FIX IS THE SAME ONE FLAG. sitemap-pseo.xml is already in ENTITY_SITEMAPS (scripts/prerender.mjs:109), whose own comment reads 'generated pSEO pages are pure SPA routes with no static fallback, so they need prerendering more than most'. Turning on PRERENDER_ENTITIES therefore covers these 244 along with the 884 entity URLs. Nothing further is needed in this repo for that half.

RECOMMENDATION, and it is a decision rather than a task: do NOT generate more pSEO pages until the prerender flag is on. Consider whether the 244 should be withheld from the sitemap in the meantime - submitting URLs that self-canonicalise to / is worse than not submitting them, and pulling them is a one-line change to the generator that can be reverted the moment prerendering lands.

AC5 and AC6 REMAIN OPEN and are genuinely not done: the inventory gate has to live where pages are generated and published (the sitemap step cannot re-count live inventory per page without N queries, as the code comment at :348 already argues), and the batched release with a GSC indexation check is blocked behind WEB-SEO-014 - Search Console is wired up but has never been synced, so there is no indexation ratio to check a batch against.

AC7 DONE 2026-08-11 (claude/prd-p1-loop) — the collision audit it asks for, run and turned into a re-runnable check. The story stays open; everything else still needs the content pipeline to run.

scripts/check-pseo-collisions.ts enumerates every URL the taxonomy can produce and diffs it against src/App.tsx routes, public/_redirects and public/sitemap-static.xml.

THE TAXONOMY'S FULL SPACE IS 3,713 URLs, not the ~950 in this story's title. Dimensions: content_type=6, location=16, audience=9, temporal=19, category=14, across the 10 patterns in src/pseo/pageTypes.ts. The ~950 in roadmap.ts is a planned subset, so AC4's "cap the initial release at ~300" is capping a much larger space than the title implies.

10 COLLISIONS WITH REAL, PRERENDERED ROUTES:
  /events/today            <- /{content_type}/{temporal}
  /events/this-weekend     <- /{content_type}/{temporal}
  /events/date-night       <- /{content_type}/{audience}
  /events/altoona          <- /{content_type}/{location}
  /events/ankeny           <- /{content_type}/{location}
  /events/clive            <- /{content_type}/{location}
  /events/johnston         <- /{content_type}/{location}
  /events/urbandale        <- /{content_type}/{location}
  /events/west-des-moines  <- /{content_type}/{location}
  /events/windsor-heights  <- /{content_type}/{location}
Every one is hand-built, in PRERENDER_ROUTES, and carries its own canonical and JSON-LD (verified while closing WEB-SEO-002 in this session). Generating a templated page for any of them would put it head to head with the real one — the WEB-SEO-007 mistake, except several hundred times over instead of once.

THE AUDIT VALIDATES ITSELF: it also reports /things-to-do/tourists, the collision that was found the hard way, shipped, and had to be 301'd to /visitors-guide under WEB-UX-016. That URL is STILL generated by the taxonomy. So the precedent AC7 points at is not merely a precedent — it is a live case the generator would recreate, and it is the control proving this check detects the class.

WHAT TO DO WITH IT: exclude those 10 (11 with tourists) from generation, or have the generator skip any URL that already resolves to a route. The second is better — the list changes whenever a dimension value or a route is added, which is why this is a script rather than a note in a story. It exits 0 and reports rather than gating, because the pipeline it guards is not wired up yet; wire it in as a hard stop when generation is switched on.

STORY REMAINS OPEN. AC1-AC3 (sitemap wiring) were already done per the prior re-scope. AC4 (cap at ~300), AC5 (the inventory gate — at least 8 qualifying events or 6 restaurants, re-evaluated per regeneration) and AC6 (batches of ~50/week, watching the GSC indexation ratio) all require running src/pseo/pipeline.ts against production, which this environment cannot do. AC5 is the control that separates this from doorway pages and should not be skipped to hit a page count.

[prior] RE-SCOPED (CODE REVIEW 2026-08-06) — the sitemap half is DONE; the reason the pages are missing has changed. public/sitemap-pseo.xml now exists and is referenced from sitemap-index.xml:32 (shipped in 1cce42f "feat(seo): put pSEO pages in a sitemap"), and scripts/generate-dynamic-sitemaps.ts:327-332 reads pseo_pages correctly. BUT the generated sitemap contains exactly ONE url — https://desmoinesinsider.com/things-to-do — against the ~950 pages this story describes. pseo_pages exists in the generated schema, and src/pseo/ carries the full system (taxonomy.ts, pageTypes.ts, pipeline.ts, componentSpecs.ts, detailedSchemas.ts, generationPrompts.ts, roadmap.ts). So the plumbing is built and routed and indexed, and the CONTENT PIPELINE HAS NEVER BEEN RUN AT SCALE — there is 1 published row. The remaining work is running/scheduling src/pseo/pipeline.ts to populate pseo_pages, not sitemap wiring.

[prior] FOUND 2026-07-29 (claude/des-moines-seo-strategy-n54bjj). Largest built-but-unshipped asset found in the repo: full taxonomy, 10 page types, generation pipeline, six-week rollout roadmap, two edge functions, live routes — and zero sitemap coverage, so the pages are unreachable except by typing the URL. SEVERITY 2 DELIBERATELY, despite the size of the opportunity: publishing a few hundred generated pages on a site whose money pages are not yet prerendered (WEB-SEO-005/006) and whose freshness pipeline does not reach crawlers (WEB-SEO-011) would be scaling a thin-content footprint on a weak foundation. The inventory gate and the batch cadence are not optional garnish here — an aggregator publishing hundreds of templated location/category permutations is the exact shape Google's doorway-page guidance describes, and the only thing distinguishing us is that our pages have real inventory behind them. Do 005, 007, 008 and 011 first. | IMPLEMENTED 2026-07-30 (claude/des-moines-seo-strategy-n54bjj), HAPPY PATH UNVERIFIED. Added generatePseoSitemap() to scripts/generate-dynamic-sitemaps.ts, registered in the Promise.all, in the generated sitemap.xml index, in the committed public/sitemap-index.xml, and in public/robots.txt. Queries pseo_pages where is_published = true, ordered by quality_score descending so the best pages survive the cap. slug is the FULL pathname with a leading slash (confirmed against fetchPseoPage in src/pseo/hooks/usePseoPage.ts) and pSEO routes span several shapes — /guide/:location, /things-to-do/*, /nightlife/* and the bare /:seg1[/:seg2] catch-alls — so the slug is concatenated directly rather than prefixed with a route. GUARDRAILS: PSEO_URL_CAP = 300 rather than the ~950 in roadmap.ts, which itself warns that a page-count spike reads as suspicious; MIN_QUALITY_SCORE = 0.6, matching the threshold PseoAdmin already treats as the borderline-acceptable line. Both exclusions are logged with counts — a capped sitemap that reports only its own size reads as full coverage, which is the failure mode the strategy doc calls out. IMPORTANT SCOPE NOTE: the quality floor is NOT the inventory gate this story asks for (>= 8 qualifying events or fold the page back up). That has to live where pages are generated and published — the sitemap step cannot re-count live inventory per page without N queries, and a page already published thin is already indexable regardless of whether it appears in a sitemap. The gate remains open work in the generation pipeline. Also added sitemap-pseo.xml to the prerender entity list (WEB-SEO-006), placed after events since pSEO pages are evergreen: they are pure SPA routes with no static fallback, so they need prerendering more than most. VERIFICATION: the Supabase host is egress-blocked in this session, so only the ERROR path could be exercised — it behaves correctly, writing a valid hub-only sitemap rather than leaving a stale file, matching the pattern established in WEB-SEO-003. Committed public/sitemap-pseo.xml carrying that single fallback URL, because robots.txt and sitemap-index.xml now reference it and a referenced-but-missing sitemap is a fetch error in GSC. The next credentialled build replaces it. PASSES STAYS FALSE until a real build confirms it emits actual pSEO URLs, and until the batch-of-50-per-week rollout with GSC indexation checks actually happens.
